        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Now I understand the structure. The `Namespace.signal` method is at line 486 in `src/blinker/base.py`. I need to add validation:\n\n1. Reject every non-string name with TypeError before dictionary lookup or modification.\n2. Accept str subclasses and the empty string.\n3. Preserve signal identity for repeated names.\n4. Preserve the original docstring.\n5. Independent names and receiver dispatch.\n\nSo the check should be `if not isinstance(name, str): raise TypeError(...)`. `isinstance` accepts str subclasses and `\"\"` is a str, so both pass.\n\nAlso, the module-level `signal` helper is just `default_namespace.signal`, so fixing `Namespace.signal` fixes both.\n\nBut wait — \"even if the namespace already contains that key\" — the check must happen before `if name not in self`. So:\n\n```python\ndef signal(self, name: str, doc: str | None = None) -> NamedSignal:\n    \"\"\"Return the :class:`NamedSignal` for the given ``name``, creating it\n    if required. Repeated calls with the same name return the same signal.\n\n    :param name: The name of the signal.\n    :param doc: The docstring of the signal.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(name, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Signal name must be a string.\")\n\n    if name not in self:\n        self[name] = NamedSignal(name, doc)\n\n    return self[name]\n```\n\nLet me check the tests to see if there's any expected error message convention. Let me look at test_signals.py for TypeError tests.",
